<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thuburbo Maius</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thuburbo Maius was a Roman town in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, about 60 kilometres south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. Originally a Punic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> settlement, it became a municipium under Hadrian and a colony under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commodus">Commodus</a>. Its ruins include a capitol, forum, baths and temples, and many of its mosaics are now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardo-national-museum">Bardo National Museum</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thuburbo-maius.jpg" alt="Thuburbo Maius" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: GIRAUD Patrick · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TUNISIE_THUBURBO_MAJUS_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The ruins of Thuburbo Maius stand in open countryside in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, with no town close to them. The site lay on a major thoroughfare that ran from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>, passing other towns such as Sbiba, Sufes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufetula">Sufetula</a>, and parts of the old Roman road survive. Its position on these trade routes made it an important settlement, and the land around it produced grain, olives and fruit.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its Roman name was Colonia Julia Aurelia Commoda. Most of the town was built between about 150 and 200, and it received a capitolium in 168. Its promotion to a municipium under Hadrian helped it grow in wealth. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crisis-of-the-third-century">Crisis of the Third Century</a> the town was restored in the 4th century. The early 3rd-century Christian martyr Perpetua was born there.</p><p>A bishopric was founded under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and survived Vandal and Byzantine rule, ceasing only with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-conquest-of-the-maghreb">Muslim conquest of the Maghreb</a>. Its known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> include Sedatus, present at the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> in 256, and Faustus, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-arles">Council of Arles</a> in 314. At the Council of Carthage of 411 the town was represented by both a Catholic bishop, Cyprianus, and a Donatist, Rufinus. Bishop Benenatus was exiled by the Vandal king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huneric">Huneric</a> in 484. The see was refounded in name in the 20th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>An excavation in 1916 uncovered a tetrastyle temple decorated with statues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, Venus, Silvanus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Bacchus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castor-and-pollux">Dioscuri</a> and a satyr, together with three perfume vases showing dogs chasing rabbits. An inscription found in 1920 in honour of C. Vettius Sabinianus showed that several other inscriptions bearing that name referred to the same man. In 1925 the House of Bacchus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariadne">Ariadne</a>, dating from the early 5th century, was excavated, and evidence of food preparation in its garden has been used to study daily life.</p><p>The town's mosaics, of the late 4th century, show a still life of foods, a sea full of fish with young people fishing from boats, and a nude Venus riding a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> among plants standing for well-being and fertility; the archaeologist Aïcha Ben Abed has studied them. None of the buildings has been fully restored, but the remains include a forum, an amphitheatre, temples, baths and houses.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thuburbo Majus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hundreds of towns flourished, from Carthage and Hadrumetum to Thugga, <b>Thuburbo Maius</b>, Sufetula, Bulla Regia, Thysdrus and Leptis Magna, with forums, baths, theatres, amphitheatres and floor mosaics of outstanding quality.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
